From af14fe9250fd4ac6e1ecf55773a1b6c924ffa145 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Klemens Arro <1125090+kempu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:30:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] email ingest: keep a message the account's Sieve script
 marked $notjunk in the Inbox

Delivery files a message the spam filter classified as spam into Junk
when it is headed for the Inbox. Since 0.16.22 a message the account's
Sieve script files with `fileinto` is delivered as ham and stays where
it was filed, `fileinto "INBOX"` included. A message the script keeps,
with `keep` or the implicit keep, is still re-routed. So a script that
marks a trusted sender's mail with `addflag "$notjunk"` and lets it be
kept, the way an organisation says "never treat this sender's mail as
spam", still sees that mail land in Junk.

Skip the re-route when the message carries $notjunk, the keyword a
script sets with `addflag "$notjunk"`. A sender cannot set a keyword
over SMTP; only the script the account owner or an administrator
installed can. Every other spam message bound for the Inbox still goes
to Junk with $junk, and nothing about classification or training
changes: the message kept in the Inbox still says X-Spam-Status: Yes.
---
 crates/email/src/message/ingest.rs | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/crates/email/src/message/ingest.rs b/crates/email/src/message/ingest.rs
index 2d2bc6c..5e1734a 100644
--- a/crates/email/src/message/ingest.rs
+++ b/crates/email/src/message/ingest.rs
@@ -354,7 +354,16 @@ impl EmailIngest for Server {
                         offset_end: extra_headers.len() as u32,
                     });
 
-                    if is_spam && params.mailbox_ids == [INBOX_ID] {
+                    // Spam headed for the Inbox goes to Junk, unless the account's own Sieve
+                    // script marked it $notjunk. A message the script filed with `fileinto`
+                    // arrives here as ham already; one it kept (`keep`, or the implicit keep)
+                    // does not, and the keyword is how the script keeps that one out of Junk.
+                    // A sender cannot set a keyword over SMTP; only the script the account
+                    // owner or an administrator installed can.
+                    if is_spam
+                        && params.mailbox_ids == [INBOX_ID]
+                        && !params.keywords.contains(&Keyword::NotJunk)
+                    {
                         params.mailbox_ids[0] = JUNK_ID;
                         params.keywords.push(Keyword::Junk);
                     }
